A||||6||Shiner||Getting ready to paint: need some advice||||5||
Z||000000||Shiner||03-13-2000||01:48 AM||||Guys, I am getting ready to paint my '87 GT.  I am going to be doing all the prep work myself, and my friend (who is a professional painter) is going to shoot it for me.  Anyway, I need some general advice on how to prep this car.  The car was originaly gold, and was repainted gold again.  It looks like they just painted right over the old stuff.  What should I do here?  Sand all the paint off?  What grades of paper should I use? I am painting the car red if that matters.<P> If anybody has done this before, LMK either on the forum here, or email me at 9monkeys@fast.net  Thanks guys||||reg||5
Z||000001||Gokart||03-13-2000||06:27 AM||||Painted my first fiero twice. I suggest you prime it first with ruddy brown. It's the best primer to be able to detect ANY imperfections to fix before laying on your red. If your in no hurry, even lay a 2nd coat of primer and plenty of wet sanding. Have fun! It is a blast!||||reg||3
Z||000002||Shiner||03-14-2000||03:52 AM||||I'll keep the Ruddy Brown in mind.  <BR>Doesn't anybody have some good advice for me??  I know there are forum members who have painted their cars!||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phil||03-14-2000||07:47 AM||||You say your friend is a professional painter, wouldn't he be the one to ask? I assume he is a spray painter not house,sign or artist type painter.||||reg||1
Z||000004||87SC/GT||03-14-2000||08:42 AM||||It is tough to give exact advice without seeing your car. A lot depends on the cars second coat of paint and how good it has adhered to the original. I would take it by an automotive paint store and let them suggest the proper products for preparation. My car had a real bad enamel paint job over the original and was peeling badly. I used a chemical stripper that was designed for urethane bumper covers. (the fiero is like painting a big bumper cover!) In most places it just removed the second paint job which allowed me to sand the original paint job with 220grit on a dual action sander. You will need a good adhesion promotor for bare spots, like "Bulldog" and several coats of 2K urethane primer. Set aside plenty of time to wet sand and do not be afraid to recoat with primer as needed to get the surface smooth.Good luck. ||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||03-14-2000||07:20 PM||||Take off as much trim as you can. Even if you will be painting it the same color as the car. You'll get a better job. Much better. Even taking the mirrors off isn't a bad idea. (Hang the drivers one so it can be moved/held out of the way while painting.) With the mirror off/loose it's really easy to get the top edge of the door between the mirror and glass to come out right and not run.<P>All loose paint must go. Use a "random orbit" sander as much as posible. Use the whole face of the disk and keep moving. you will dig or flat spot Fiero body pannels very easily. You do not have to sand to the bare pannel, but you do want to get into the original paint/primer on any areas that are flaked, chipped, blistered, etc.<P>220 is about the coarsest you want or need for this car. even then use it at a minimum. Get the loose stuff off then move on. Don't try to feather much with it. You have to always keep in mind that the paint is close to the same strength as the pannel. The pannel may even be softer than the paint depending on what one it is.<P>Once you are sure ALL the loose junk is gone. then sand the entire surface of the car. A fast pass with 400 then another with 600 or finer. This will do 3 things. It will break the surface of the old paint and it will remove all the old waxes/polishes. Even after this use a chemical prep product. If you leave any polishes behind they will screw with the new paint. Third thing it does is flatten all the old over spray bumps and anything else fine sticking to the surface.||||reg||1
Z||000006||jhar1987||03-14-2000||10:29 PM||||I have sprayed two fieros. one was a repaint using base coat clear coat. on this one i sanded the whole car with 400 grit,then wiped it clean. i did not have to use a primer on this just shot base color over what i had. but this was the same color. only thing i added was flex to the paint to keep it from cracking. the other one i shot in enamel,i sanded the car with 400 grit wiped it down gave the whole car a good coat of primer with flex added. wet sanded the primer with 600grit and shot the color.this car was silver and i went to white.in your case prep the car like by sanding but try not to heat up the panels to much when sanding. shot the car with a good coat of primer with flex wet sand with 600-1000grit wipe it down and shoot your color. i waited a week wet sanded again and buffed the car.it looks like glass.||||reg||1
